= Solution
{parent=i}

Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ suggests $\phi=xy+g(y,z)$. Matching the other components gives $g=\sin(yz)+C$. Direct differentiation verifies
$$
\boxed{\phi_1=xy+\sin(yz)+C,\qquad\mathbf F_1=\nabla\phi_1.}
$$
Thus the <vector field> is <conservative>, with the displayed <scalar potential>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The $z$ component of the <curl> is $\partial_xF_y-\partial_yF_x=e^{x+y}-xe^{xy}$, which is not identically zero; at the origin it is one. Hence **the field is not conservative on $\mathbb R^3$** and has no global <scalar potential>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Integrating the first component and checking the remaining <partial derivatives> gives
$$
\boxed{\phi_3=x^2yz-\cos x+C,\qquad\mathbf F_3=\nabla\phi_3.}
$$
This explicit <scalar potential> proves that the field is <conservative>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Parametrize the straight path by $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\leq t\leq\pi$. Then $\mathbf F_3\cdot d\mathbf r=(4t^3+\sin t)dt$, so direct evaluation of the <line integral> gives
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F_3\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_0^\pi(4t^3+\sin t)dt=\pi^4+2.}
$$
The <fundamental theorem for line integrals> supplies an independent check: this is $\phi_3(\pi,\pi,\pi)-\phi_3(0,0,0)$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the first segment only $z$ varies and $F_z=x^2y=0$. On the second only $y$ varies and $F_y=x^2z=0$. On the last segment $y=z=\pi$, so the <line integral> is
$$
\boxed{0+0+\int_0^\pi(2\pi^2x+\sin x)dx=\pi^4+2.}
$$
The agreement is the expected <path independence> of a <conservative vector field>.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
