= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Where $f_z\ne0$, the <implicit function theorem> makes the surface a local graph $z=h(x,y)$ with $h_x=-f_x/f_z$ and $h_y=-f_y/f_z$. Its upward <vector area element> is $(-h_x,-h_y,1)dx\,dy$. Therefore the <vector area element of an implicit surface> is
$$
\boxed{d\mathbf S=\frac{\nabla f}{f_z}\,dx\,dy,\qquad\mathbf F=\frac{\nabla f}{f_z},\quad\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf k=1.}
$$
Dividing by $f_z$ fixes the upward orientation even if the initially chosen gradient points downward. The restriction excludes a locally vertical graph, where this particular projected-area representation cannot be used.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose $f=x^2(1+y)+y^2z-1$. Since $f_z=y^2>0$ on the stated open domain, its upward <vector area element> is
$$
\boxed{d\mathbf S=\left(\frac{2x(1+y)}{y^2},\ \frac{x^2+2yz}{y^2},\ 1\right)dx\,dy,\qquad z=\frac{1-x^2(1+y)}{y^2}.}
$$
The graph becomes unbounded near parts of $y=0$; the flux integrals below are understood as limits from $y>0$.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Subtract the integrands before integrating: $\mathbf G_2-\mathbf G_1=(0,y^3,0)$. With the upward <vector area element>,
$$
(\mathbf G_2-\mathbf G_1)\cdot d\mathbf S=(x^2y+2y^2z)dx\,dy=(2-2x^2-x^2y)dx\,dy.
$$
This extends continuously to the lower edge, so
$$
\boxed{|I_1-I_2|=\left|\int_0^1\int_0^2(2-2x^2-x^2y)dy\,dx\right|=2.}
$$
The individual <flux integrals> also converge: $\mathbf G_1\cdot d\mathbf S=x(3+2y)dx\,dy$ gives $I_1=5$, and hence $I_2=7$. Reversing the surface orientation changes both signs and leaves the requested magnitude unchanged.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
