= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the plane $x=0$, the right half of the cross-section has coordinates $y=a(1-\cos\theta)\sin\theta$, $z=a(1-\cos\theta)\cos\theta$, $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$. Reflect across the $z$ axis for the other half. The <cardioid> has a cusp at $(y,z)=(0,0)$ for $\theta=0$, crosses $z=0$ at $(\pm a,0)$ for $\theta=\pi/2$, and reaches $(0,-2a)$ for $\theta=\pi$. The small upper lobes reach $z=a/4$; the widest section is below the equator.

\Image[paper-2-apple-cross-section.png]
{title=Cardioid cross-section and a horizontal slice of the apple-shaped solid}
{description=Original plot in units of $a$. The shaded lower portion has polar angles above $\pi/2$. The thin marked slice is bounded by horizontal planes, rather than by rays from the origin.}

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For <horizontal slices of a surface of revolution in spherical coordinates>, use disk area times vertical thickness. Here the cylindrical radius is $\rho=a(1-\cos\theta)\sin\theta$ and the height is $z=a(\cos\theta-\cos^2\theta)$. For $\theta>\pi/2$, $dz/d\theta=-a(1-2\cos\theta)\sin\theta<0$. Hence a positive angular increment gives
$$
\boxed{dV=\pi a^3(1-\cos\theta_0)^2(1-2\cos\theta_0)\sin^3\theta_0\,d\theta.}
$$
These are horizontal disks in the lower part of the solid. A spherical angular-wedge element $r^2\sin\theta\,dr\,d\theta\,d\phi$ describes a different infinitesimal slice.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Integrate the horizontal-disk expression over $\pi/2<\theta<\pi$. With $u=\cos\theta$ and $\sin\theta\,d\theta=-du$,
$$
V=\pi a^3\int_{-1}^0(1-u)^2(1-2u)(1-u^2)du=\pi a^3\int_{-1}^0(1-4u+4u^2+2u^3-5u^4+2u^5)du.
$$
Evaluating the <integral> of this <polynomial> gives
$$
\boxed{V=\frac{5\pi a^3}{2}.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
