= Solution
{parent=a}

With $M=2xy^2+4$ and $N=2x^2y-6$, the cross derivatives agree: $M_y=N_x=4xy$. This is an <exact first-order ordinary differential equation>. Integrating $M$ with respect to $x$ gives a potential $x^2y^2+4x+g(y)$; matching $N$ gives $g'=-6$. The implicit solution is
$$
\boxed{x^2y^2+4x-6y=C.}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here $M_y=2y$ whereas $N_x=0$, so the equation is not exact. The identity $M_y-N_x=N$ gives the <integrating factor for a differential one-form> $\mu(x)=e^x$. Multiplying makes the left-hand side the <exact differential> of $e^x(y^2-x+1)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{e^x(y^2-x+1)=C,\qquad\text{or}\qquad y^2=x-1+Ce^{-x}.}
$$
For real graph solutions, the right-hand side must be nonnegative; a smooth choice of square-root branch is made on the interval concerned. Finding the factor did not require dividing by $y$ at zero.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The cross derivatives are both $2y$, so the equation is exact. The function $x\cos x+xy^2$ differentiates to the given one-form. Hence
$$
\boxed{x\cos x+xy^2=C.}
$$
Differentiating this implicit equation checks both coefficients of the <exact differential> directly.

