= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assuming the egg colours are <independent> with the same probability $p$, the number of white eggs has the <binomial distribution> with four trials. The possibilities are

|| Contents
|| Probability
| $W_0B_4$
| $(1-p)^4$
| $W_1B_3$
| $4p(1-p)^3$
| $W_2B_2$
| $6p^2(1-p)^2$
| $W_3B_1$
| $4p^3(1-p)$
| $W_4B_0$
| $p^4$

The <binomial coefficients> count which eggs are white. Marginal colour probabilities alone would not determine nest probabilities without the <independence> assumption.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $p=3/4$, the five <probabilities> in increasing white-egg order are $(1,12,54,108,81)/256$. The largest is $108/256=27/64$, so the **most common nest has three white eggs and one brown egg**, $W_3B_1$.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <expected value> of the discrete <uniform distribution> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X]=\frac{1+2+3+4+5+6}{6}=\frac72.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Using the <sum of squares>, the second <moment> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X^2]=\frac{1+4+9+16+25+36}{6}=\frac{91}{6}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <variance> is the second <moment> minus the square of the <expected value>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{91}{6}-\left(\frac72\right)^2=\frac{35}{12}.}
$$

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The function is a <probability density function>, rather than a cumulative distribution: its integral over both intervals must be one. Thus $2\alpha+2\alpha=1$ and
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac14.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The density is symmetric about $7/2$, giving <expected value> $\mathbb E[X]=7/2$. Its second <moment> is $\mathbb E[X^2]=\frac14(\int_1^3x^2dx+\int_4^6x^2dx)=89/6$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{89}{6}-\frac{49}{4}=\frac{31}{12}.}
$$
Equivalently, each equally weighted interval has variance $1/3$ and its centre is $3/2$ from the overall mean, giving $1/3+9/4$ by the <law of total variance>.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Separate variables away from the equilibrium solutions. Partial fractions give
$$
\frac1{a-b}\ln\left|\frac{y-a}{y-b}\right|=-x+C,\qquad\frac{y-a}{y-b}=K e^{(b-a)x}.
$$
Thus the <constant-coefficient Riccati equation> has real solution family
$$
\boxed{y=\frac{a e^{ax}-bK e^{bx}}{e^{ax}-K e^{bx}},\quad K\in\mathbb R,\qquad\text{together with }y=b,}
$$
on intervals where the denominator is nonzero. The value $K=0$ includes $y=a$. Setting $K=e^{(b-a)c}$ recovers the printed expression after factoring out $e^{ac}$. For real $c$ that substitution covers positive $K$ only; negative $K$ gives additional real branches between the equilibria, and the constant solutions must not be discarded when separating variables.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the printed family, put $D(x)=e^{a(x+c)}-e^{b(x+c)}$. Its <logarithmic derivative> is $D'/D=y$, so $z'=yz$ integrates to
$$
\boxed{z=C_1\bigl[e^{a(x+c)}-e^{b(x+c)}\bigr].}
$$
This includes $z=0$ and is valid on each interval on which the corresponding $y$ is finite. For the full real family, write $z=Ae^{ax}+Be^{bx}$ and $y=z'/z$, with $(A,B)\ne(0,0)$. The pure exponentials include the two equilibrium values of $y$; arbitrary relative coefficient signs include the branches missing from real $c$ in the printed expression.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

When the two roots coincide, $y'=-(y-a)^2$. Separation gives $y=a+1/(x+c)$, and the equilibrium $y=a$ must be added. Integrating $z'=yz$ gives
$$
\boxed{y=a+\frac1{x+c},\quad z=C_1(x+c)e^{ax};\qquad\text{or}\qquad y=a,\quad z=C_1e^{ax}.}
$$
The nonconstant pair is restricted to an interval excluding $x=-c$. Equivalently every nonzero linearizing function is $z=(A+Bx)e^{ax}$, with $y=a+B/(A+Bx)$ wherever its denominator is nonzero.

= d
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Differentiate $z'=yz$ without dividing by $z$:
$$
z''=(y'+y^2)z=[(a+b)y-ab]z=(a+b)z'-abz.
$$
Thus the <linearization of a Riccati equation> gives
$$
\boxed{z''-(a+b)z'+abz=0.}
$$
Its <characteristic polynomial> is $(\lambda-a)(\lambda-b)$, explaining both the distinct-root exponentials and the repeated-root factor $A+Bx$. Nonzero solutions reconstruct $y=z'/z$ between their zeros; the identically zero $z$ also satisfies the linear equation.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <convergent series> $\sum_{n\geq1}u_n$ has <partial sums> $S_N=\sum_{n=1}^Nu_n$ tending to a finite limit $S$. Explicitly, for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is an integer $N_0$ such that $|S_N-S|<\varepsilon$ whenever $N\geq N_0$. The convergence of the individual terms to zero is necessary but is not sufficient for convergence of the series.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <telescoping series> has partial sum $S_N=w_{N+1}-w_1$. Therefore the necessary and sufficient condition is **that $w_n$ have a finite limit** $L$. The sum is then $L-w_1$.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <comparison test for series> states that if $0\leq u_n\leq v_n$ for all sufficiently large $n$, convergence of $\sum v_n$ implies convergence of $\sum u_n$. Equivalently, divergence of the smaller nonnegative series forces divergence of the larger one. Finitely many initial terms do not affect either convergence conclusion.

= d
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Subtract the right side from the left and write $g(x)=1-(1+x)^{-p}-px(1+x)^{-p-1}$. Then $g(0)=0$ and $g'(x)=p(p+1)x(1+x)^{-p-2}>0$ for $x>0$, so the strict inequality follows. Set $x=1/n$ and multiply by $n^{-p}>0$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{n^{-p}-(n+1)^{-p}>p(n+1)^{-p-1},\qquad p,n>0.}
$$
This decreasing-power difference supplies a <telescoping bound for a p-series>.

= e
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Put $p=k-1>0$. The preceding inequality bounds $n^{-k}<[ (n-1)^{-p}-n^{-p}]/p$ for $n\geq2$. For $N\geq2$, summing the <telescoping series> gives
$$
\sum_{n=1}^Nn^{-k}<1+\frac{1-N^{-(k-1)}}{k-1}<1+\frac1{k-1}.
$$
These nonnegative partial sums increase and are bounded, so the <monotone convergence theorem for sequences> proves **convergence for every $k>1$**. This is the <telescoping bound for a p-series>, an alternative to the <integral test>.

= 11
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Applying the <multivariable chain rule> at fixed $y$ gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_y=\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}\right)_v\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}\right)_y+\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial v}\right)_u\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\right)_y.}
$$
The held-fixed variables distinguish the two coordinate descriptions; the same scalar function is being differentiated.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiate the first-order <chain rule> again, including derivatives of the coordinate coefficients. The <second derivatives under a two-variable coordinate change> are
$$
\boxed{f_{xx}=f_{uu}u_x^2+2f_{uv}u_xv_x+f_{vv}v_x^2+f_u\,u_{xx}+f_v\,v_{xx},}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{f_{yy}=f_{uu}u_y^2+2f_{uv}u_yv_y+f_{vv}v_y^2+f_u\,u_{yy}+f_v\,v_{yy}.}
$$
The last two terms account for derivatives of the coordinate functions; they vanish for an <affine map>. Smoothness justifies equality of the two mixed partial derivatives.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For <polar coordinates> with $u>0$, $\partial_x=\cos v\,\partial_u-(\sin v/u)\partial_v$ and $\partial_y=\sin v\,\partial_u+(\cos v/u)\partial_v$. Write $c=\cos v$, $s=\sin v$. Applying these operators twice, including their variable coefficients, gives the <Cartesian second derivatives in polar coordinates>:
$$
\boxed{f_{xx}=c^2f_{uu}-\frac{2sc}{u}f_{uv}+\frac{s^2}{u^2}f_{vv}+\frac{s^2}{u}f_u+\frac{2sc}{u^2}f_v,}
$$
$$
\boxed{f_{yy}=s^2f_{uu}+\frac{2sc}{u}f_{uv}+\frac{c^2}{u^2}f_{vv}+\frac{c^2}{u}f_u-\frac{2sc}{u^2}f_v.}
$$
In particular their sum is the <Laplacian in polar coordinates>, $f_{uu}+f_u/u+f_{vv}/u^2$.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The substitution gives $x^2-y^2=u^2\cos2v$. For this function $f_{uu}=2\cos2v$, $f_u/u=2\cos2v$, and $f_{vv}/u^2=-4\cos2v$, whose sum is zero. Thus it satisfies the polar form of <Laplace's equation> for $u>0$. The apparent coordinate singularity at $u=0$ does not make the original Cartesian <polynomial> singular; it is a smooth <harmonic function> through the origin.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $D=1+x^2+y^2$. The <gradient> has components $f_x=[D-2x(x+y)]/D^2$ and $f_y=[D-2y(x+y)]/D^2$. At a <critical point>, subtraction gives $(x-y)(x+y)=0$. The case $x+y=0$ cannot make either numerator zero, so $x=y=t$. The remaining equation is $1+2t^2=4t^2$, giving
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(1/\sqrt2,1/\sqrt2),\quad f=1/\sqrt2;\qquad(x,y)=(-1/\sqrt2,-1/\sqrt2),\quad f=-1/\sqrt2.}
$$
These are the only stationary points; the denominator is positive everywhere.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At $(t,t)$ with $t^2=1/2$, differentiating the <gradient> yields the <Hessian matrix> $H=-tI$. At the positive point it is <negative definite>, and at the negative point it is <positive-definite>; in both cases $f_{xy}=0$ and $\det H=1/2$. Therefore the **positive point is a maximum, the negative point is a minimum, and there are no saddle points**.

They are global extrema: with $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives $|f|\leq\sqrt2r/(1+r^2)\leq1/\sqrt2$, with equality at exactly those two points. This is the extremum bound underlying the <level sets of a linear numerator over a radial quadratic>.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a nonzero level $h$, completing the square gives
$$
\boxed{\left(x-\frac1{2h}\right)^2+\left(y-\frac1{2h}\right)^2=\frac1{2h^2}-1.}
$$
Thus the <level sets of a linear numerator over a radial quadratic> are circles centred on $y=x$, real only for $0<|h|\leq1/\sqrt2$. The extreme levels shrink to points. The zero contour is the straight line $x+y=0$, separating positive and negative heights; near it, small nonzero levels are large circles. Heights tend to zero at infinity.

\Image[paper-2-rational-height-contours.png]
{title=Circular nonzero contours around the two extrema of a rational height function}
{description=Original contour plot. Blue positive and orange negative circles surround the marked maximum and minimum; the black line is the zero contour. Circle centres lie on $y=x$.}
